LESSONS = [
L("Chapter 1 — The security constitution: passwords, 2FA & sessions", "13", "<p>The final module opens where the platform's defence begins: the SECURITY CONSTITUTION — the site-level settings that decide how hard every door is to force, walked as the track's last settings walk. The security chapter.</p><p><b>The password layer.</b> The policy as configuration, per module 2's oldest lesson: the PASSWORD rules enforced by the machinery, not the memo (the minimum strength, the complexity posture, the reuse and rotation stance — each field a policy the settings make physics: the weak-password site is not a user failure but a configuration choice someone made by not making it), with the trade's realities addressed head-on: the SHARED password convicted one final time (module 7's concurrent-session signature having shown how it is CAUGHT; this chapter states how it is PREVENTED — one person, one account, enforced by the lifecycle, with the request channel fast enough that sharing has no excuse: the workaround retired by service, the standing cure), and the password MANAGER posture stated for the credential-holding seats (the custody of Chapter 6 of the connections module extended to human passwords — governed storage over memory and sticky notes, for the accounts that matter most).</p><p><b>The second factor.</b> The 2FA discipline, prioritised honestly: the second factor MANDATORY on the powerful accounts first (the administrative account, the configure-class holders, the payment-adjacent and tightest-tier seats — the accounts whose compromise is a firm-level event carrying the protection proportional to that fact: the admin account with only a password is the master key under the doormat), EXTENDED by risk tier rather than blanket decree (the rollout sequenced — the powerful first, the sensitive-data seats next, the general population as the firm's maturity allows: the sequencing being an honest trade-scale judgment, because the 2FA rollout that starts with everyone often ends with no one after the first week's friction), and the RECOVERY paths governed (the lost-device procedure through the channel with identity verification — the 2FA reset request being a favourite social-engineering door, and the verification discipline being the answer: the reset that requires only a phone call has re-installed the doormat).</p><p><b>The session layer & the device reality.</b> The remaining constitution: the SESSION policies (the expiry balancing security against the counter's rhythm — the till account that logs out mid-queue breeds the workaround that never logs out: the tiers differentiated, the powerful accounts' sessions short, the operational accounts' sized to the shift), the SIMULTANEOUS-session posture decided per module 7's signature, and the DEVICE reality named for the trade: the shared branch machines (the terminal three people use across a shift — the lock-screen habit between users, the personal-account-on-shared-device hygiene, the browser's saved-password trap on communal machines: the device layer being where site security meets shop-floor reality, and the branch walk-through being part of the security constitution's verification — the fences of module 2 mean less if the till's browser remembers the manager's password). The chapter's summary for the site book: <b>strength enforced by settings, the second factor on every powerful door, sessions sized to their risk, and the shop floor walked — the constitution that makes the easy attack expensive.</b></p>"),
L("Chapter 2 — The audit layer: versions, trails & the site's memory of acts", "13", "<p>Six tracks relied on trails; module 2's was-it-used and module 4's first recovery rung both READ one; this chapter GOVERNS it: the audit layer — the site's memory of who did what, when. The evidence chapter.</p><p><b>The layer's anatomy.</b> The trails, named: the VERSION history (the document's tracked changes — every edit's before-and-after, the recovery ladder's first rung and the dispute's quiet resolver), the ACTIVITY trail (the acts on a document — created, submitted, cancelled, by whom, when: the workflow history module 5 protected, the approvals' evidence), the ACCESS records (the views and exports on the sensitive tiers — module 4 of this track's sampling source), and the LOGIN history (module 7's silhouette source) — together the site's testimony, and the chapter's first discipline is that the testimony is CONFIGURED deliberately: tracking enabled on what MATTERS (the transactional doctypes, the masters, the sensitive tiers — decided with the functions at implementation: the audit configuration being a policy walk like every constitution in this academy), not on everything indiscriminately (the track-everything site drowns its own testimony in noise and storage — the audit layer sized to the evidence needs, per the functions' laws and the adviser's horizons), and VERIFIED as on (the constitution walk's check — the version history someone assumes exists, discovered absent during the dispute that needed it, being the untested-backup disease at the evidence layer: the audit configuration proven, not presumed, which module 4's rung-1 usage does quarterly for free).</p><p><b>Reading the trail properly.</b> The evidence discipline, consolidated from its scattered appearances: the trail read with the SAME epistemics as every register (the acts attributed to ACCOUNTS, with module 7's account-versus-person boundary held — the trail says which login did what; the person behind the login is an investigation's conclusion, not a log line's), the was-it-used question answered from here (module 2's open-fence finding weighted by this layer's answer — the trail turning 'the door was open' into 'the door was open and unused' or 'and used, twice, in March': the severity's whole difference), the trail's GAPS honest (what the layer does not record, known — the read that leaves no trace where access logging is not configured: the sampling of module 4 designed around the layer's actual coverage, never an imagined one), and the trail ITSELF protected (the audit records' own integrity — the trail nobody can quietly edit being the point: the platform's design carrying this, and the admin's duty being not to break it — the log-cleaning 'tidiness' that deletes activity history is destroying evidence, refused with module 3's bulk-delete firmness).</p><p><b>The retention & the testimony's horizons.</b> The layer's calendar: the trails retained per the EVIDENCE horizons (the same adviser-confirmed reach as the documents they testify about — the version history that ages out before its document's dispute window is testimony that resigns early), the storage cost of memory priced honestly (module 7's waistline including the trails' growth — the retention conversation, again, governed by the functions and the horizons, never by tidiness), and the layer's quiet economics closing the chapter: the audit trail costs storage and configuration once — and pays in every dispute shortened, every investigation grounded, every was-it-used answered, and every recovery's first rung standing: the site that remembers its acts is the site whose word means something, which is six tracks' entire evidence philosophy, installed at the layer where acts actually live.</p>"),
L("Chapter 3 — The incident procedure: when something is wrong", "13", "<p>The suspected breach, the confirmed anomaly, the compromised credential — the moments the platform's calm breaks. Met by procedure, not improvisation. The incident chapter.</p><p><b>What is an incident.</b> The definition drawn deliberately: an INCIDENT is any event where the platform's INTEGRITY is in question — the credential suspected leaked (the phishing message someone may have answered, the key found in the wrong place), the access anomaly that survived module 7's innocent explanations, the data apparently altered outside process, the door apparently forced — distinguished from the OUTAGE (module 4's terrain — the system down is continuity; the system untrusted is an incident: different runbooks, sometimes both at once), and the threshold deliberately LOW for declaring one (the incident declared and closed cheap beats the incident debated while it grows — module 7's pre-written thresholds feeding this door, and the culture point stated: the person who reports the phishing click within minutes is the hero of the story, and the firm that punishes the report teaches the next click to hide for three weeks, which is the entire game lost in one HR decision).</p><p><b>The procedure.</b> Five verbs, in order: <b>CONTAIN</b> (the suspected credential rotated NOW, the suspected account disabled NOW — containment before investigation, because the open question with an open door is compounding: the rotation that proves unnecessary cost minutes; the investigation that ran while the door stood open cost whatever walked through it), <b>PRESERVE</b> (the evidence captured before it churns — the logs exported, the timestamps recorded, the affected records noted: Chapter 2's trail read and SAVED, because investigation alters the scene it walks through), <b>INVESTIGATE</b> (the questions in order — what happened, through which door, touching what, since when: the trail's testimony assembled with module 7's evidence discipline and module 2's account-versus-person boundary held throughout), <b>REMEDIATE</b> (the door fixed, the damage assessed and repaired through the functions' own machinery — the altered records corrected by their owners' correction processes, per every track's law: incident recovery does not suspend document governance), and <b>LEARN</b> (the post-incident review — module 4's pattern: what let this happen, what caught it, what the runbook missed: the procedure amended while memory is fresh).</p><p><b>The communication duties.</b> The incident's people layer: the OWNER informed by severity (the threshold for the MD's phone ringing decided in calm — the owner who learns of the incident from the story's third act has a governance grievance on top of a security one), the AFFECTED functions told what they need (the tier whose data was in question told so, with the facts — the payroll seat learning its register was exposed from a corridor rumour being the communication failure that costs more trust than the incident), the BOUNDARY engaged where the platform layer is implicated (the host's security channel, per the map), and the EXTERNAL duties flagged honestly (the data-protection obligations where personal data was exposed — the adviser's terrain, the notification questions LEGAL questions answered by counsel, not by hope: the admin's duty being the facts assembled and the escalation prompt, never the legal judgment). The chapter's closing frame, in the academy's oldest cadence: the incident procedure is the deceased-employee procedure of the platform layer — written in peacetime, rehearsed in tabletop, and carrying everyone through the day nobody wanted: the firm with the procedure has a bad day; the firm without it has a bad quarter.</p>"),
L("Chapter 4 — The watched watcher: the admin's own governance", "13", "<p>Seven modules have said it in passing; this chapter says it as the standard: the admin — holder of every key — is the firm's most-governed user, not its least. The conduct chapter.</p><p><b>The standard, consolidated.</b> The rules gathered from their scattered statements into one page: the TWO-ACCOUNT discipline (module 1 — the daily self under ordinary fences; the powerful self used deliberately, briefly, loggedly: the admin doing daily work from the administrative account has made every browsing moment a privileged act), the SELF-GRANT ban (module 2 — the admin's own access changed only by another's authorisation and hands), the PLATFORM-ACTS-ONLY law (modules 1 and 4 of this track — the keys used for administration, never for reading what the fences guard: the postman's ethics of the communications module, formalised: the admin who CAN see everything and is TRUSTED because they demonstrably don't look), the DATA-ACT prohibition given its teeth (the admin's access to the sensitive tiers LOGGED by Chapter 2's layer and the log READ by someone else — the monthly sample of module 7 pointing at the administrative account first, because the watcher watched is the whole design), and the CONFLICT declarations (the admin's relative in the workforce, the admin's own records — the acts touching them performed by the coverage or witnessed, per the nobody-files-their-own-case law at its final desk).</p><p><b>Nobody audits their own keys.</b> The principle's machinery: the admin's OWN access review as the permission walk's standing last page (module 2 — reviewed and signed by the MD or the designated second: the one page of the walk the admin does not self-certify), the admin's acts SAMPLED by the same second (the quarterly look at the administrative account's activity — brief, standing, normalised: the review that exists is the review that deters, and the admin who requested the review's existence has made the strongest possible statement about their own conduct — the certified admin ASKS for the watching, because the watched watcher is trusted with more, promoted safer, and suspected never: module 5 of the payroll track's argument, at the seat with the most keys), and the second's role honest at trade scale (the MD, the accountant, or the external partner's periodic review — the perfect independence unavailable at eleven people, the adequate version entirely available: someone who is not the admin, looking, on a rhythm, with the right to ask).</p><p><b>The temperament, named.</b> The chapter's closing portrait, because the standard is lived by a person: the role rewards the temperament the academy has described seven times — the precision that reads before acting, the patience that refuses the corridor, the humility that routes rather than fixes, the steadiness that runs the same checklist in the incident as in the drill — and the failure modes are temperamental too (the admin who enjoys the power is the wrong admin; the hero who fixes everything at 2 a.m. and documents nothing is building the single-head module 6 exists to prevent; the curious browser of other people's data is the breach already in progress), with the selection guidance stated for every implementation: the best trade-scale admin is almost never the most technical person available — it is the most GOVERNED person available, taught the technical, which is a curriculum (this one); the reverse is a temperament transplant, which is not.</p>"),
L("Chapter 5 — The sysadmin calendar: every rhythm, one page", "13", "<p>Seven modules installed rhythms; the consolidation is the operating system — the academy's seventh and final calendar. The page the platform runs on.</p><p><b>Daily — the glance.</b> Five minutes, four silhouettes: the error log's shape, the scheduler's heartbeat and queue depth, the login pattern's anomalies, the email queue's failures (modules 6-7) — the tick that keeps every invisible layer visible.</p><p><b>Weekly — the read and the page.</b> The error triage to a known state (the new species named, the clusters routed), the backup completion glance with the size trend (module 4's silent-failure detector), and Friday's HEALTH PAGE assembly (module 7 — eight lines, fifteen minutes, the week as facts with thresholds standing).</p><p><b>Monthly — the registers.</b> The dormant-account scan (module 2), the access-register-to-HR reconciliation (the ghost's homelessness maintained), the storage dot on the waistline (module 7), the compliance doors' reconciliations supported (module 6's issued-minus-accepted read with the owning functions), and the sensitive-tier access sample (the administrative account first, read by the second — module 4 of this module).</p><p><b>Quarterly — the proofs.</b> The RESTORE DRILL (module 4 — the calendar's most important line, the checklist full, the log signed), the notification and reminder TEST-FIRES (modules 5-7's insurance), the dedup hunt and quality reads (module 3 — the four numbers per master type), the credential register's rotation review (module 6), and the tabletop incident rehearsal annually rotated through scenarios (module 3 of this module — the procedure exercised before it is needed).</p><p><b>Annually — the constitutional season.</b> Scheduled across weeks, never stacked: the PERMISSION WALK (module 2 — the leaver scan, the roles walked empirically, the admin's own page signed by the second), the SETTINGS and SECURITY constitution walks (modules 1 of the track and of this module — every default re-confirmed as a decision), the SERIES and customisation register audits (modules 3, 5 — the unexplainable retired), the INTEGRATION reviews (module 6 — each door justifying its hinges), the RETENTION and plan reviews (module 4 — the RPO/RTO re-priced, the runbook copies re-versioned), the health thresholds re-priced (module 7 — the normals moved with the firm), and the SITE BOOK's own walk (module 1 — the eight sections read, the stale corrected, the copies outside refreshed).</p><p><b>The page's law, seventh and final telling.</b> Every line a NAME (at trade scale: the admin's hat, the second's review lines, the functions' owned reconciliations — three or four names covering the page), the page sized honestly (the daily is minutes; the weekly a half-hour; the quarterly an afternoon; the annual a season of scheduled mornings — the four-hour hat of module 1, fully accounted), and the sentence the academy has now earned seven times, at the layer beneath the other six: <b>the calendar with owners IS the implementation</b> — every disaster this track described (the untested backup, the silent scheduler, the sprawled permissions, the stale runbook, the unwatched watcher) was a calendar line that had no name, and the certified admin's final deliverable is this page, because the platform governed by rhythms is a function, and the platform governed by moods is a countdown.</p>"),
L("Chapter 6 — The handover test: the platform survives its keeper", "13", "<p>The academy's deepest thread, at its seventh and final surfacing: could the platform survive its administrator? The handover chapter.</p><p><b>The test, platform edition.</b> Could a competent successor, given what exists, hold the hat within a month with ZERO questions answerable only by the person who left? The walk, module by module: the SITE BOOK's eight sections (module 1 — the boundary map orienting the relationships, the configuration record explaining every setting's why), the ACCESS machinery legible (module 2 — the role catalogue readable aloud, the register reconciling, the walk's reports showing the method), the MASTER governance documented (module 3 — the custodianship map, the import logs, the series catalogue), the CONTINUITY standing (module 4 — the runbooks reachable, the drill log proving the machinery, the RPO/RTO signed), the CHANGE story readable (module 5 — the log with its how-to-reverse lines, the customisation register with its purpose sentences), the CONNECTIONS owned (module 6 — the inventory, the credential register, the communication map), the HEALTH normals inherited (module 7 — the baselines documented, the thresholds written, the page's history orienting in an hour), and this module's structure holding the rest. Every NO is a module incompletely done — the test is the audit; the audit is the goal: the seventh telling, verbatim by design.</p><p><b>The admin-in-one-head disease, closed.</b> The single-head's final conviction, with the platform's specific severity: the undocumented admin is the firm's ENTIRE operational memory in one resignation letter — the passwords in one head, the customisations' reasons in one memory, the incident procedure in one set of reflexes: the departure (or the illness, or the dispute) that takes the head takes the firm's ability to safely operate, change, or recover its own system — and the trade's actual pattern named honestly: the 'IT person' who left two years ago and the site nobody has dared touch since (the frozen site being the disease's monument — improvements stopped, updates feared, the firm operating a system it no longer understands: every consultant has walked into one), with the cure being this track ITSELF, said one final time: the eight modules ARE the extraction — the head's contents converted into the book, the registers, the runbooks, and the calendar a governed successor can hold.</p><p><b>The absence test & the succession.</b> The proof and the plan: the admin's planned ABSENCE with the hat held by the coverage (the mandatory-leave control pointed at its final target — the two-week test where the glance, the weekly read, and whatever the fortnight brings run on the documents: the drill of module 4 having already rehearsed the coverage's hardest scenario, the rest being the book's readability proven live), the COVERAGE named and developed in advance (the second — often the same second who reads the admin's logs: the deputy pattern of the HR track at the platform seat, with the coverage's development being literally this curriculum), and the handover CEREMONY defined for the real transition (the walk-through of the eight sections, the credentials rotated INTO the new custody — never copied, rotated: the leaver-admin's every ambient possibility closed by the same sweep any leaver gets, plus the administrative account's own succession — the shadowed period, and the sign-off by the second: the platform changing hands like the controlled function it now is). The chapter's closing sentence, for the seat itself: <b>the admin whose platform survives them has converted the firm's scariest technical dependency into its most boring — and has freed themselves in the process: documented custodians take holidays, get promoted, and are trusted with more, which is the deal this academy has offered every seat it certified, honoured one last time.</b></p>"),
L("Chapter 7 — The owner's platform page & the seat's certification", "13", "<p>What the owner should know, ask, and receive about the platform — and how the seat itself is certified. The accountability chapter.</p><p><b>The owner's platform page.</b> The quarterly digest formalised (module 7's four lines, given their standing): the BACKUP line (drills current, last date, findings closed — the 'are we safe?' answer), the SECURITY line (the walk's date and finds, incidents open or zero, 2FA coverage on the powerful accounts), the CAPACITY line (the headroom in months, the growth's slope), and the CHANGE line (the quarter's changes logged, rollbacks exercised, the update stream current) — four lines, each answerable BY POINTING (the drill log, the walk report, the waistline chart, the change log: the owner's platform questions all having documents behind them being this track's whole achievement, stated as a page), plus the owner's ASKING duty stated plainly (the questions the MD should ask quarterly and expect pointed answers to: when was the last restore drill and what did it find? when was the last permission walk and who signed your page? — the owner who asks these twice a year keeps the whole layer honest at the cost of two conversations, and the owner who never asks has delegated their insurance to hope).</p><p><b>The seat's certification.</b> The admin certified the way every seat in this academy is: the LITERACY demonstrated (the boundary map explained, a permission diagnosis walked live, the recovery ladder narrated with its rungs' reasons), the RITUALS observed (a restore drill run under observation, a change taken through the full ceremony, a health page assembled — the go-live gate's platform half), and the standard MAINTAINED by the artifacts (the drill logs, the walk reports, the change log's how-to-reverse lines, the health page's history — the file that proves the platform monthly ALSO proving the professional holding it: the self-assembling appraisal folder of the payroll track, at its final seat), with the certification's renewal implicit in the calendar (the seat that keeps the rhythms keeps the certificate's meaning — the lapsed calendar being the lapsed certification in practice, whatever the paper says).</p><p><b>The consultant's platform lens.</b> The chapter's professional close, for the certified consultant who now holds this track beside the functional six: the platform read added to the walk-in diagnostic (the prospect's site assessed in an hour by this track's questions — the site book's existence or absence, the admin population's size, the last drill's date if any, the change log's existence, the error log's staleness: the five-question platform health check that prices the foundation before any function is assessed, because the functional remediation built on an ungoverned platform is a renovation on an unsurveyed foundation), and the engagement pattern honest (the platform track's findings usually sequence FIRST in remediation — the custodian named, the access rebuilt, the backup proven — because every functional fix assumes the floor: the sequencing instinct the seven-track graduate carries, and Chapter 8 demonstrates). The chapter's one-line summary: the platform is certified the way everything in this academy is certified — by artifacts, rhythms, and answers that point — and the owner, the seat, and the consultant each hold their page of it.</p>"),
L("Chapter 8 — Case study: the platform year closes — and the seven-track graduate", "16", "<p>The final case study of the seventh track, in two acts: HealthTrade's platform becomes structure that survives everyone, and the academy's graduate — seven certifications deep — walks a firm one last time.</p><p><b>Act one — the structural year.</b> <b>Q1, the constitutions (Ch. 1-2):</b> the security walk lands 2FA on the powerful accounts first (the administrative account, the configure-class holders, the payroll seat — eleven days, zero drama, the sequenced rollout working as designed), the session tiers differentiated (the till accounts sized to the shift; the powerful accounts shortened), the branch device walk-through retiring two saved-password traps on communal machines — and the audit configuration verified deliberately (one gap found: version tracking absent on a sensitive master, enabled through the ceremony; the trail proven, not presumed). <b>Q2, the incident (Ch. 3):</b> the year's test arrives as a phishing message the accountant ALMOST answered — reported within minutes (the hero-of-the-story culture paying immediately), the procedure run by its verbs: contained (the credential rotated inside the hour, the sessions cleared), preserved (the logs exported, timestamped), investigated (the trail showing no successful entry — the was-it-used answer clean), remediated (nothing to repair), learned (the tabletop scenario updated with the real message's anatomy, the report-fast culture reinforced at the branch meeting by thanks, not blame). The MD's note: <i>we had our first security incident and it was boring. I understand now that boring was the entire purchase.</i> <b>Q3, the watched watcher and the absence (Ch. 4, 6):</b> the admin's quarterly log review by the MD normalises into routine (the ops lead having requested it herself in Q1 — the strongest statement, made), and the absence test runs in month eight: two weeks' leave, the hat held by the accountant-second on the book — the glance kept, the health page assembled twice, one scheduler stall handled by the runbook's three-line diagnosis and pre-answered restart path, the return to a clean page. Her note closes the arc that began in module 1: <i>the floor survived its keeper. The book held. Apparently that was the point all along.</i> <b>Q4, the year seals (Ch. 5, 7):</b> the calendar's first full cycle completes (the annual season run across five scheduled mornings — the walk, the constitutions, the audits, the reviews), and the owner's platform page stands beside the six functional pages: drills current, walks signed, incidents zero-open, headroom fourteen months.</p><p><b>Act two — the seven-track graduate.</b> The consultant holding all seven certifications takes the complete call one final time: <i>we want to computerise — everything is chaos.</i> The walk now reads the firm AND its floor: the six functional lenses find their familiar diseases (the undocumented counter, the unverified gate, the phantom stock, the unclosable books, the spreadsheet workforce, the remittance hole) — and the PLATFORM lens completes the survey in five questions: no site book, six administrator accounts, no drill ever, no change log, an error log two years unread — the foundation priced before the functions, and the remediation sequenced by the full curriculum: <b>the floor first</b> (the custodian named, the access rebuilt, the backup proven — weeks one to four, because every functional fix assumes it), the people and pay layers on the governed floor, the money and goods layers on their foundations — and the graduate's proposal closes with the sentence seven tracks earned: <i>I will leave you a firm that runs on documents, pays on structure, proves itself monthly — and stands on a floor with a named keeper, a tested recovery, and a book that survives us all.</i> That consultant — able to read a firm's counter, gate, warehouse, books, people, payslips, AND the platform they all stand on, price its diseases, and repair them in the right order from the foundation up — is what the complete academy exists to produce. Take the assessment; then go build firms that outlive their heroes — every layer of them.</p>"),
L("Chapter 9 — Patterns, the eighth law & the academy at seven", "14", "<p>The structural layer's final scar tissue, the law that closes the track — and the academy, complete at seven.</p><p><b>Pattern 1 — the weak-door site.</b> Symptom: passwords by preference; the admin account with no second factor; the shared credential living in the sessions. Disease: the security constitution never walked (Ch. 1). Fix: strength as settings, 2FA on the powerful doors first, sessions tiered, the shop floor walked.</p><p><b>Pattern 2 — the testimony assumed.</b> Symptom: the version history discovered absent during the dispute that needed it; the audit layer's coverage imagined, not known. Disease: the trail presumed, never proven (Ch. 2). Fix: the audit configuration walked as a constitution, verified empirically, retained per the evidence horizons.</p><p><b>Pattern 3 — incident improvisation.</b> Symptom: the phishing click hidden for three weeks; the credential debated while the door stands open; the owner learning from the third act. Disease: no procedure, and a culture that punishes reports (Ch. 3). Fix: the five verbs written in peacetime, the low declaration threshold, the reporter as the story's hero, the tabletop rehearsed.</p><p><b>Pattern 4 — the unwatched watcher.</b> Symptom: the admin nobody audits; the powerful account used for browsing; the keys' holder outside every fence. Disease: the most-governed seat governed least (Ch. 4). Fix: the two accounts, the self-grant ban, the logs read by the second, the admin's own page signed by another — the watching the certified admin requests.</p><p><b>Pattern 5 — the admin-in-one-head.</b> Symptom: the IT person who left and the site nobody has dared touch since; the frozen system the firm no longer understands. Disease: the platform's memory in one resignation letter (Ch. 6). Fix: the track itself as the extraction — the book, the registers, the runbooks, the calendar — and the absence test as the proof.</p><p><b>Pattern 6 — the hatless site.</b> Symptom: everyone and no one administering; the floor unowned while six functions govern above it; every platform question answered 'whoever set it up'. Disease: the first law never installed (Ch. 5, and module 1). Fix: the custodian named, the calendar with names, the rhythms owned — the platform as a governed function among governed functions.</p><p><b>The eighth law.</b> The custodian named, the fences proven, the masters governed, the survival drilled, the evolution ceremonied, the voice owned, the watching installed — and now the whole layer stands in structure: <b>administration lives in structure — a security constitution walked, testimony configured and proven, incidents met by five rehearsed verbs, the watcher watched by a named second, every rhythm on a calendar with owners, a platform that survives its keeper by the book it wrote governing itself, and an owner who can ask four questions and be answered by pointing.</b></p><p><b>The seventh track — and the academy at seven.</b> Eight modules: the custodian (1), the fences (2), the masters' machinery (3), the survival (4), the change (5), the voice and doors (6), the watching (7), the structure (8). And the academy now stands at SEVEN tracks — <b>Selling, Procurement, Inventory, Accounts, HR, Payroll</b>, and beneath them all, <b>Sysadmin</b> — 56 modules, 56 laws, seven continuous case studies through one firm's whole anatomy AND the floor it stands on, and the conviction told in seven vocabularies: <b>documents over memory, masters over improvisation, rhythms over heroics, structure over trust — at every layer, including the one the layers run on.</b> The professional who holds all seven can walk into any trading firm in Nigeria and read it whole — from the counter to the configuration — price its diseases from the foundation up, sequence its cure floor-first, and leave it able to run, hire, pay, prove, and recover itself without them. That is the complete certification, completed. Take the assessment; then go read a business — all of it, and everything it stands on.</p>"),
]

